 "web": "These barriers land on the same people at once. Take one woman in a canyon colonia. She assembles medical devices on a formal contract with IMSS coverage, so the floor exists, but her wage does not cover the rent near the plant (Barrier 4). So the family built on a slope with no drainage (5). They have no deed, so the house cannot back a loan for a retaining wall, and the cement is bought with a small personal loan at more than 50% a year (9, 10). Its runoff goes to the same river that closes the beach where the Mass is held (6). Her brother was deported and sleeps in the canal (2). Her neighbor's children are in the home where RJ students stay, because their mother works night shifts and the grandmother died (3). A visa to visit the brother's U.S.-born children costs more than she can save and may be refused (8). No one barrier decided her life. Together they set the price. *(A composite for teaching, not a person. Do not present it as someone students met.)*",
